{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the streets like confetti. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, the bitterness balanced by the sweetness of wagashi sweets. In Gion, I caught a glimpse of a geisha gliding down an alley, her wooden geta clicking softly against stone.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of contrasts. In Shibuya, crowds surged through the scramble crossing like a living river; moments later, I sat alone in a Zen garden in Meiji Shrine, listening to wind chimes and silence. I ate ramen at 2 a.m. in a tiny alleyway shop, slurping noodles under the glow of paper lanterns while the owner nodded in quiet approval.\n\nThe bullet train to Hiroshima was a marvel—smooth, swift, silent. At the Peace Memorial Park, I placed a paper crane beside hundreds of others, each carrying a wish for peace. That evening, I wandered Miyajima Island as the torii gate glowed crimson against the tide, its reflection shimmering like a bridge to another world.\n\nEverywhere, I noticed the quiet respect: strangers bowing without words, shopkeepers wrapping purchases with deliberate care, even the way trash was neatly sorted on the street. Japan doesn’t shout; it whispers—and you learn to listen.\n\nI left with a small wooden fox from Fushimi Inari, a notebook full of haiku, and the lingering taste of yuzu in my tea. No photos captured the chill of morning mist over Arashiyama’s bamboo grove, or the warmth of an elderly woman offering me an onigiri because I looked lost. \n\nJapan didn’t just show me its sights—it taught me stillness. In a world that never stops, I finally learned how to pause.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.22264541685581207}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.060953639447689056}, {"toke